  "result": "Tool calling is like a person in a sealed office who can only pass notes through a slot.\n\nThey can't leave the room, so they can't check the booking calendar themselves. Taped to the door is a menu of services: \"Booking desk: tell me a machine and a day, I'll say if it's free.\" They write a note: \"Booking desk: is the laser cutter free Tuesday?\" A runner outside carries the note to the desk, does the lookup and slips the answer back under the door. The person reads it and either writes another note or writes the final reply to the student.\n\n- **The model** is the person. It only writes notes. It never touches the calendar.\n- **Your program** is the runner. It does the actual work.\n- **The tool description** is the menu. It's the only thing the model knows about what's available.\n\nThe analogy breaks at one point: the runner is code you write, and nobody decides for them what to do with a note.",
